-- title:
--   Fine-level Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation with Atkin–Lehner and level lifts
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $r$ and $\bar r$ are distinct primes, $N$ a nonzero natural number with $r \nmid N$, $\bar r \nmid N$ and $N$ squarefree (`hrr`, `hrN`, `hrbarN`, `hN`).
--
--   **Coefficient rings.** $\mathcal O$ is a characteristic-zero domain which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`) in which $2$ is invertible (`h2`), $\pi \in \mathcal O$ is irreducible, $\mathcal O$ is $(\pi)$-adically complete, $\#(\mathcal O/\pi) = r$ and $(r) = (\pi)$ in $\mathcal O$ (so $\mathcal O$ is absolutely unramified with residue field of order $r$); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Next, $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ (written `Onr`) is a characteristic-zero domain and $\mathcal O$-algebra, $\mathrm{Fr}$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism of it, subject to: $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^{r} \bmod \pi$ for all $x$ (`hFr`). Finally $\mathrm{vdet} : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is a monoid homomorphism characterised by `hvdet`: $\mathrm{vdet}(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$, i.e. $\mathrm{vdet}$ is the $\pi$-adic valuation of the determinant.
--
--   **Indefinite side and moduli packages.** $a, b \in \mathbb Q$ satisfy `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b r rbar`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for every finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ the algebra $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies over $r$ or over $\bar r$; $\Lambda$ is a maximal order (an order maximal among orders) in $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$. The scheme $\mathcal X$ with structure morphism $f$ to $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal O$, together with the point assignment `pt` sending each ring $S$, each morphism $s : \mathrm{Spec}\,S \to \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal O$ and each fake elliptic curve with $\Gamma_0(N)$-level datum over $S$ to a morphism $\mathrm{Spec}\,S \to \mathcal X$ over $s$, is a coarse moduli package (`h𝒳` : `IsCoarseModuli`, whose five clauses are invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with pullback of fake elliptic curves along ring maps, surjectivity and injectivity up to isomorphism on points over algebraically closed fields, and the universal property among such packages).
--
--   Further, $n \ge 3$ with $r \nmid n$, $\bar r \nmid n$ and $\gcd(n,N) = 1$; $M \to \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal O$ with assignment `ptF` on pairs (fake elliptic curve, full level-$n$ structure) is a fine moduli package (`hM` : `IsFineModuli`: isomorphism-invariance, pullback compatibility, and surjectivity and injectivity of `ptF` on $S$-points for every ring $S$). A group $G$ with $\rho : G \to \mathrm{Aut}\,M$ and $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ satisfies `hρ` : `IsLevelTwistAction` (six clauses: $\rho$ acts over the base; $\rho(h)$ transports `ptF` along twists of the level structure by $\chi(h)$; and $\chi$ is multiplicative, unital, surjective and injective modulo $n$). The morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ lies over the base ($p \mathbin{;} f = f_M$), is $\rho$-invariant, and forgets the full level structure on points (`hp_pt`).
--
--   **Tower and Atkin–Lehner data.** For each prime $\ell$ different from $r$ and $\bar r$ (the type `HeckeTower.AwayPrime r rbar`) there is $\mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal O$ with an assignment `ptT` on pairs (fake elliptic curve, extra level-$\ell$ subgroup scheme) forming a coarse moduli package `IsCoarseModuliT` (same five clauses, for pairs), together with two morphisms $d_0, d_1 : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over the base such that $d_0$ forgets the extra level (`hd₀`) and $d_1$ sends a pair to any target of an $\ell$-level isogeny out of it (`hd₁`, via `FakeEllipticCurve.IsLevelIsogeny`). Morphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r} : \mathcal X \to \mathcal X$ over the base implement the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $r$ respectively at $\bar r$ on points: whenever $E'$ is an Atkin–Lehner quotient of $E$ at $r$ (resp. $\bar r$) in the sense of `FakeEllipticCurve.IsAtkinLehnerQuotient`, the point of $E$ followed by $a_r$ (resp. $a_{\bar r}$) is the point of $E'$.
--
--   **Definite side.** $a_1, b_1 \in \mathbb Q$ satisfy `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt rbar`: $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$, and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra exactly for $v$ over $\bar r$. Here $\Lambda_1$ is a maximal order, $R_1$ an Eichler order of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$ in one of them) with $R_1 \le \Lambda_1$; $n_1$ is a unit of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb A_{\mathbb Q}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ lying in `primeHeckeSet R₁ r` (its adelic value lies in the finite-adelic box of $R_1$, $r$ times its inverse lies in that box, while neither its inverse nor $r^{-1}$ times it does), and $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ (`meetOrder R₁ n₁`) is an Eichler order of level $Nr$ (`hS₁`). Also $\iota_0 : \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$ is an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map, $v$ is the height-one prime of $\mathbb Z$ containing $r$, and $\tilde\Gamma$ (`Γt`) is the subgroup of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^{\times}$ consisting exactly of the elements lying, at every prime $w \ne v$, in the subgroup generated by the local box units of $R_1$ at $w$ (`CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v`).
--
--   For each $\ell$ away from $r, \bar r$ there are $s_\ell \in \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^{\times}$ and an adelic unit $sf_\ell$ with (`hs`): $sf_\ell$ has component $s_\ell$ at every prime not containing $r$ and component $1$ at the primes containing $r$; the product of the diagonal idele of $\ell$ with $sf_\ell^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₁ (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` if $\ell \mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` otherwise; and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$. Moreover $\tilde\Gamma(\ell) := \tilde\Gamma \cap s_\ell \tilde\Gamma s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`). Finally $\bar w$ (`wbar`) is a unit of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\tilde\Gamma$ (`hwbar`), and, by the integrality hypothesis `hwR`, some power $r^{k}$ times $\bar w$ lies in $R_1$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exist a group homomorphism $\tilde\theta : \tilde\Gamma \to G$, a family
--   $$\Theta_f : \bigl((O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} B) \times \hat\Omega(B)\bigr) \times G \longrightarrow \widehat M(B),$$
--   defined for every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent — here $\hat\Omega(B) =$ `(Omega K₀ π).obj B` is the set of Deligne data over $B$ (a $B$-submodule of each base-changed full lattice with invertible quotient, monotone, homothety-equivariant and nondegenerate at every prime of $B$) and $\widehat M(B)$ is the set of morphisms $\mathrm{Spec}\,B \to M$ over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal O$ — and families $\Theta_{T,\ell} : (O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} B) \times \hat\Omega(B) \to \widehat{\mathcal Y_\ell}(B)$ for each $\ell$ away from $r, \bar r$, such that the following thirteen assertions hold.
--
--   (1) $\Theta_f$ is natural: for every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\varphi : B \to B'$ between $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebras, $\Theta_f$ of the transported datum equals the transport of $\Theta_f$ of the datum.
--
--   (2) $G$-equivariance: for all $x$, $g$, $h$, the automorphism $\rho(h)$ applied to $\Theta_f(x, gh)$ gives $\Theta_f(x, g)$.
--
--   (3) $\tilde\Gamma$-invariance: for $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ and $x, x'$ related by `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` at $\iota_0(\gamma)$ — that is, $x'$ has unramified coordinate $x_1 \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0\gamma)}$ and Deligne datum the pullback of $x_2$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$ — one has $\Theta_f(x', \tilde\theta(\gamma) g) = \Theta_f(x, g)$.
--
--   (4) Formal étaleness: for a surjective $\mathcal O$-algebra map $p : B \to B_0$ between $\pi$-nilpotent algebras whose kernel is square-zero (any two elements killed by $p$ have product zero), for every datum $x_0$ over $B_0$ and every $B$-point $y$ of $M$ whose image over $B_0$ is $\Theta_f(x_0)$, there is a unique datum $x$ over $B$ with image $x_0$ and $\Theta_f(x) = y$.
--
--   (5) Geometric fibres: for every algebraically closed field $k$ that is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent, and all $\psi, \psi' : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} k$, $P, P' \in \hat\Omega(k)$, $g, g' \in G$: $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g) = \Theta_f((\psi',P'),g')$ if and only if there is $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ with $g' = \tilde\theta(\gamma) g$, with $P'$ the pullback of $P$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$ (`DeligneDatum.IsPullback`), and with $\psi'(y) = \psi(\mathrm{Fr}^{-\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0\gamma)}(y))$ for every $y \in O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ that is fixed by $\mathrm{Fr}^{\mathrm{vdet}(\iota_0 z)}$ for all rational scalars $z \in \tilde\Gamma$ with $\tilde\theta(z) = 1$.
--
--   (6) Each $\Theta_{T,\ell}$ is natural in $B$ in the same sense.
--
--   (7) $\tilde\Gamma(\ell)$-invariance: for $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma(\ell)$ and $x, x'$ related by the twisted action at $\iota_0(\gamma)$, $\Theta_{T,\ell}(x') = \Theta_{T,\ell}(x)$.
--
--   (8) Geometric fibres of $\Theta_{T,\ell}$: over an algebraically closed $\pi$-nilpotent $k$ and for a fixed $\psi$, $\Theta_{T,\ell}(\psi,P) = \Theta_{T,\ell}(\psi,P')$ if and only if some $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma(\ell)$ relates $(\psi,P)$ and $(\psi,P')$ by the twisted action at $\iota_0(\gamma)$.
--
--   (9) Level-$\ell$ fine lifts: for every $\ell$ away from $r, \bar r$ and every level-$\ell$ fine package, namely $M_\ell \to \mathrm{Spec}\,\mathcal O$ with an assignment `ptFℓ` on triples (fake elliptic curve, full level-$n$ structure, extra level-$\ell$ subgroup) satisfying `IsFineModuliT`, a morphism $\pi_\ell : M_\ell \to M$ over the base compatible with the assignments, a morphism $p_\ell : M_\ell \to \mathcal Y_\ell$ over the base compatible with the assignments, and an action $\rho_\ell : G \to \mathrm{Aut}\,M_\ell$ over the base with $\rho_\ell(h)$ followed by $\pi_\ell$ equal to $\pi_\ell$ followed by $\rho(h)$, fixing $p_\ell$, and realising the twist by $\chi(h)$ on `ptFℓ` whenever an isomorphism $e$ exhibits the twist and matches the extra level structures (`hρℓtw`) — there exists $\Theta_{f,\ell}$ with values in $\widehat{M_\ell}(B)$ satisfying: naturality; $G$-equivariance for $\rho_\ell$; invariance under those $\gamma$ lying in $\tilde\Gamma$ and in $\tilde\Gamma(\ell)$, in the form $\Theta_{f,\ell}(x', \tilde\theta(\gamma) g) = \Theta_{f,\ell}(x,g)$; formal étaleness as in (4); the geometric fibre description as in (5) with the extra requirement $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma(\ell)$; and the two compatibilities $\Theta_{f,\ell}$ followed by $\pi_\ell$ equals $\Theta_f$, and $\Theta_{f,\ell}(\,\cdot\,, 1)$ followed by $p_\ell$ equals $\Theta_{T,\ell}$.
--
--   (10) First degeneracy: $\Theta_{T,\ell}(x)$ followed by $d_0$ equals $\Theta_f(x,1)$ followed by $p$.
--
--   (11) Second degeneracy: if $x'$ and $x$ are related by the twisted action at $\iota_0(s_\ell)$ (in this order), then $\Theta_{T,\ell}(x)$ followed by $d_1$ equals $\Theta_f(x',1)$ followed by $p$.
--
--   (12) Atkin–Lehner at $r$: $\Theta_f(x,1)$ followed by $p$ and then by $a_r$ equals $\Theta_f((x_1 \circ \mathrm{Fr}, x_2), 1)$ followed by $p$; that is, $a_r$ acts by a single Frobenius twist of the unramified coordinate, the Deligne datum being unchanged.
--
--   (13) Atkin–Lehner at $\bar r$: if $x, x'$ are related by the twisted action at $\iota_0(\bar w)$, then $\Theta_f(x,1)$ followed by $p$ and then by $a_{\bar r}$ equals $\Theta_f(x',1)$ followed by $p$.
--
--   No surjectivity of $\Theta_f$, $\Theta_{T,\ell}$ or $\Theta_{f,\ell}$ on geometric points is asserted, and no universal property of quotients by $\tilde\Gamma$ or $\tilde\Gamma(\ell)$ is asserted; the fibre clauses (5), (8) and the corresponding clause of (9) are injectivity statements only.
--
--   This is the Čerednik–Drinfeld $r$-adic uniformisation of the Shimura curve of fake elliptic curves with $\Gamma_0(N)$-structure, in the form used in the formalisation: a family of formal points of the fine level-$n$ moduli scheme parametrised by the formal $r$-adic upper half plane twisted by the unramified coordinate, equivariant for an arithmetic group $\tilde\Gamma$ coming from a definite quaternion algebra, compatible with the Hecke degeneracy maps at primes away from $r$ and $\bar r$, with the Atkin–Lehner maps at $r$ and $\bar r$, and lifting to the fine moduli schemes with extra level $\ell$. It feeds the version of the uniformisation that adds geometric connectedness and surjectivity, on the way to the description of the special fibre and the reduction of the Jacobian used in level lowering.
